    {
      "id": "be-s11-rothschild-lerner-c1",
      "heading": "The Rothschild index",
      "explanation_md": "**R = ET / EF**\n\n- **ET**: elasticity of demand for the **total market**.\n- **EF**: elasticity of demand for **one firm's** product.\n\nIf there are many similar firms, raising **your** price sends buyers to rivals, so **EF is large** and R is **close to 0** (perfect competition). If you are the only firm, your demand **is** the market demand, so EF = ET and **R = 1** (monopoly).\n\n**Application:** it tells a manager how much pricing power firms in the industry have. In the slide's table, Tobacco and Chemicals have R = 1.00; Food is only 0.26.",
      "example_ids": [
        "be-ex-rothschild-table"
      ],
      "key_terms": [
        {
          "term": "Rothschild Index",
          "meaning": "R = ET / EF, between 0 (perfect competition) and 1 (monopoly)",
          "source_ref": "be--session-11#10"
        }
      ],
      "exam_line": "R = ET / EF; 0 is perfect competition, 1 is monopoly.",
      "clarification": null,
